🇪🇺 EU Equities
Index performance:
– STOXX Europe 600: +6.3% YTD
– EURO STOXX 50: +7.5% YTD
– Trailing the S&P 500 due to limited tech exposure and higher sensitivity to global trade headlines

Sector drivers:
– Industrials and energy are leading EU gains
– Autos, luxury, and exporters saw pullbacks on tariff news but rebounded after US–EU truce
– Tech underweight remains a structural drag relative to US peers

Technical & sentiment:
– Brief corrections in July did not trigger trend reversal
– No strong flows in or out — JPM notes relatively stable positioning across European equity funds
– Momentum is slowing but remains positive

JPM tactical view:
– Neutral on broad EU equities
– Favor cyclical industrials over defensives and exporters
– Watching for relative opportunities (e.g., Spain over Italy)



🌏 EM Asia Equities
Index performance:
– MSCI EM Asia: +7.1% YTD
– MSCI Taiwan: +11.4% YTD
– Nifty 50 (India): +8.7% YTD
– Outperformed other EM regions, led by tech and domestic growth names

Sector/market leaders:
– Semiconductors and hardware tech (Taiwan, Korea) fueled Q2 rally
– India driven by infrastructure and domestic demand stories
– ASEAN markets mixed — Vietnam strong, Thailand lagging

Flow dynamics:
– Net outflows of $6.8B in July per EPFR data — mainly from passive EM funds
– Rotations ongoing toward India and Indonesia, away from China-sensitive exporters

JPM tactical view:
– Medium-weighted on EM Asia equities
– Favor Asia ex-China, particularly India and Taiwan
– Maintain hedged exposure due to FX and trade risks; cautious on EM momentum sustainability into 2H



Index performance:
– S&P 500: +12.4% YTD as of late July
– Strongest among major developed markets
– Led by mega-cap tech, resilient earnings, and AI investment optimism

Sector/style drivers:
– Growth +14.8%, Quality +13.1%, Value +9.2% (YTD)
– AI infrastructure and chip stocks led gains in tech-heavy Growth/Quality baskets
– Energy, banks, and cyclicals supported Value, but lagged in relative terms
– Momentum factor underperformed, hinting at early rotation

Technical & sentiment:
– JPM notes positioning is long but not extreme:
– CTA (trend-following funds) remain net long but not overextended
– Retail flows have moderated since June
– No evidence of “euphoria” or blow-off top
– Volatility expected to rise post-summer as positioning is rebalanced

JPM tactical view:
– Selectively bullish, especially in Growth and Quality stocks
– Cautious on chasing recent winners; prefers balanced exposure
– Watching for signs of capex fade in tech as 1H spending may have been front-loaded due to tariff risk
– No shift in equity overweight yet, but rebalancing may be warranted in 2H if earnings slow or margins compress






#### Rates
🇺🇸 US Treasuries
Market performance:
– Yield curve steepened slightly in July
– 10Y yield: ~4.25% (JPM YE25 forecast: 4.35%)
– 2Y yield: ~4.45% → projected to fall to 3.50% by YE25 as Fed begins cutting

Macro & supply drivers:
– Q2 GDP beat (+3.0%), but final sales only +1.1% → Fed pause still justified
– Sticky core PCE inflation (2.8%) slows timing of cuts
– Q3 net T-bill issuance projected at $667B → adds steepening pressure to curve
– Labor market cooling, but not collapsing; soft landing priced in

Technical & sentiment:
– JPM notes positioning is balanced — no strong bias toward duration
– Real money and foreign flows stable, but issuance remains heavy
– Fed funds futures price >80% chance of first cut in September, though JPM expects December

JPM tactical view:
– Neutral duration overall
– 5s/20s curve steepeners remain preferred expression
– Avoid long 2Y for now; modest long bias on the 10Y into year-end
– Treasury volatility (MOVE Index) expected to rise into September refunding window

🇪🇺 Eurozone (Bunds & Peripherals)
Market performance:
– 10Y Bund yield around 2.60% (JPM stop loss: 2.72%)
– Peripheral spreads narrowed: Spain and Portugal outperforming Italy

Macro drivers:
– Inflation moderating; ECB paused in July
– JPM expects first cut in October
– EU–US tariff truce lowers macro uncertainty short-term

JPM tactical view:
– Long 10Y Bunds as core duration hedge
– Intra-EMU: Long Spain vs Germany and Italy
– Valuations attractive relative to global peers

🇬🇧 UK Gilts (SONIA market)
Market positioning:
– Market priced aggressively for BoE cuts
– Short-end rallied; front-end SONIA very flat

JPM tactical view:
– Flattener trades in SONIA: e.g., 1Y1Y/3Y1Y
– Potential restrike of 2s/10s steepeners after summer

🇯🇵 Japan (JGBs)
BoJ stance:
– Maintaining yield curve control and easing
– Inflation projections slightly raised, but no hike bias yet

JPM tactical view:
– Curve flatteners preferred (long 10Y vs 30Y)
– Avoid outright long duration due to FX pressure

🌍 EM Local Rates
Favored markets:
– Overweight (OW) in: Malaysia, Mexico, Hungary, South Africa
– All offer attractive real yields and dovish policy shifts expected
– Malaysia CPI slowed to 1.8% y/y, supporting BNM cuts

JPM tactical view:
– Maintain long duration in select EM local curves
– FX risk warrants tight stop-losses or local currency hedges


#### FX and Commodities
💱 FX (Foreign Exchange) — As of Aug 1, 2025
💵 USD (DXY Index)
Performance: Slight rebound post-month-end; DXY back above 104

Drivers:
– US Q2 GDP strong, but private demand weakening
– Sticky core PCE (2.8%) + tariffs = stagflation concerns
– US curve steepening adds mild FX support

JPM View:
– ⚖️ Neutral ST, 🔻 Bearish MT
– Fed still expected to cut in December, not September
– JPM still targets USD weakness into Q4 as growth slows

💶 EUR/USD
Performance: Holding above 1.095 despite dollar strength

Drivers:
– EU–US trade truce limited tariff damage to EU autos
– PMIs stable; ECB paused

JPM View:
– 🔺 Mild bullish bias remains
– Target: EUR/USD ~1.15 by year-end

💴 JPY
Performance: Steady at ~143/USD

Drivers:
– BoJ still dovish; no hike, but market watching wage data
– Global equity vol hasn’t triggered strong safe haven flow

JPM View:
– ↔ Neutral
– No active trade; remains defensive hedge if vol spikes

🌍 EM FX
Performance: Diverging — MXN, ZAR firm; BRL, IDR weaker

Drivers:
– Rising US yields and tariffs weighing on EMFX
– Equities saw $6.8B outflows in July, weakening FX flows

JPM View:
– ⚖️ Medium-weight, with a hedged bias
– Favor MXN, ZAR (carry + policy clarity); avoid BRL, IDR

🛢️ Commodities — As of Aug 1, 2025
🛢️ Oil (Brent / WTI)
Performance: Choppy; Brent trading near $84/bbl

Drivers:
– OPEC+ maintaining cuts
– US demand mixed; inventories drew down slightly

JPM View:
– 🎢 No directional bias, favors trading vol
– Range-bound with upside tail risk from supply disruptions

🟫 Copper
Performance: Plunged ~6.5% on July 30, now near $3.60/lb

Drivers:
– US finalized 50% tariff on Chinese copper and intermediate metal products
– Traders fear retaliation from China or demand slowdown
– LME/COMEX arbitrage widened intraday but snapped back

JPM View:
– ⚠️ Tactically bearish / underweight
– Watching closely for China policy response and EM demand fallout
– Would re-enter long only on confirmation of stimulus or supply cuts

🪙 Gold
Performance: Flat at ~$1,970/oz

Drivers:
– Fed cut expectations stalled until Dec
– Real yields still high; demand soft

JPM View:
– 🕒 Waiting for catalyst
– No active long yet; would turn bullish if yields fall or vol rises sharply

🌾 Agriculture (Grains / Softs)
Performance: Still oversold, but stabilizing

Drivers:
– Speculative positioning at multi-year lows
– No major supply news

JPM View:
– ⚠️ Oversold, potential tactical bounce
– Prefer optionality or risk-reversal longs over outright



#### Currency
💵 USD (DXY)
Macro momentum fading: While headline Q2 GDP surprised to the upside, underlying private demand softened and core PCE stayed sticky at 2.8%, fueling stagflation concerns.

Positioning and policy: Broad USD short positioning has unwound recently, but JPM sees scope for further dollar weakness as the Fed remains on track for a December cut.

JPM View: Neutral short-term, bearish medium-term; prefers USD shorts vs EUR and CAD, and USD-funded longs in SEK/NOK.

💶 EUR/USD
Resilient data: Stabilizing PMIs and the easing of EU–US tariff tensions helped EUR stay firm above 1.095, even as USD rebounded.

Policy expectations: The ECB paused in July and maintains a dovish tone, with one final 25bps cut likely in October.

JPM View: Mildly bullish, holding EUR longs tactically, targeting EUR/USD ~1.15 into year-end.

💴 JPY/USD
JPY remains range-bound near 143, with BoJ dovish and no safe-haven flows materializing despite global uncertainties; JPM stays neutral but sees JPY as a defensive hedge.

💷 GBP
UK labor data is weakening and JPM expects BoE to begin cutting rates on August 8; tactically short GBP vs EUR, SEK, and NOK.

🇨🇭 CHF
Supported by relative yield and macro resilience, CHF remains favored vs GBP and neutral vs EUR.

🇨🇦 CAD
JPM maintains short USD/CAD as positioning cleaned up and oil-linked fundamentals remain stable.

🇦🇺 AUD
AUD sentiment turned cautious after copper’s sharp drop and RBA’s dovish lean; JPM closed AUD longs and is now on the sidelines.

🇳🇿 NZD
With mixed jobs data and fading export momentum, JPM holds a neutral view on NZD near term.

🇳🇴 NOK & 🇸🇪 SEK
Scandi FX favored for carry and relative value; JPM is long NOK/SEK vs GBP and EUR.

